---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Duluth, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Duluth, GA 30096 and 30097. Houses, townhomes, and condos. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Duluth" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can about
      the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Duluth</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Duluth</h2>
    <p>
      Duluth is two places. Downtown is Town Green and Parsons Alley, which were
      rebuilt in the 2010s with shops, restaurants, apartments, and some older
      houses around them. The other Duluth is around Pleasant Hill Road and I-85,
      where Gwinnett Place Mall used to be. The mall has been closed since 2021,
      and people still give directions off it. Peachtree Industrial, which
      everyone calls PIB, runs through the middle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Duluth has more attached housing than most of Gwinnett. There are
      townhomes, condos, and apartments along PIB and Pleasant Hill, and a lot of
      them have a unit upstairs and a unit below. That changes the usual call. It
      is often a leak in one unit that ends up in another.
    </p>
    <p>
      The typical house in Duluth was built around 1992. That is old enough for
      the water heater, the washer hose, and the supply lines to be on their
      second round or past it. In the single-family houses, the usual call is a
      water heater or a hose. In the attached housing, it is water that came
      through a ceiling.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under the house matters too. Around Pleasant Hill and Gwinnett
      Place, many older homes and condos are on a slab or a crawl space. In the
      gated country club neighborhood, basements are common. Tell us what you
      have, and if you are not sure, say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your home. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. If it is a townhome or condo, tell us which floor
      you are on and whether the water came from a unit next to you or above you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. In a January 2022 storm, a tree came down near
      Cardinal Lake. And during Hurricane Helene in 2024, photos showed water on
      Indian Trail near I-85. A damaged roof can leak into the attic for days
      before the stain shows up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>
      Duluth has several ZIP codes. 30096 and 30095 cover most of the city. The
      gated country club neighborhood is usually listed as 30097.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Town Green and Parsons Alley.</strong> The rebuilt downtown.
      Apartments, shops, and some older houses nearby.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pleasant Hill and Gwinnett Place.</strong> Condos, townhomes, and
      single-family houses from the 1980s and 1990s. Many people here rent.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Peachtree Industrial (PIB).</strong> Townhomes and two-story
      houses. This is where you see the most upstairs-and-downstairs leaks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sugarloaf Country Club and Sugarloaf Park.</strong> Gated estate
      houses from the late 1990s and 2000s, with basements. Sugarloaf Park is
      smaller and next door. This is not the same place as Sugarloaf Mills, which
      is in Lawrenceville.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cardinal Lake Estates.</strong> Older, with more than 650 modest
      houses around a lake.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Duluth</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Leak from the unit above.</strong> A burst supply line, an
        overflowing tub, or a failed water heater upstairs ends up in the ceiling
        below.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the 1980s and 1990s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. In December 2022, a burst pipe left the Villas at Duluth
        apartments without water for days.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Outside water at ground level.</strong> In heavy rain, water can come
        in under a door on the ground floor, especially near Indian Trail and
        Pleasant Hill.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm damage.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a
        roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Duluth homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          I own a townhome and the leak started in my unit. Do I call the HOA or
          handle it myself?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Look at your HOA documents, or call the management company, and ask who
          is responsible for what. In many townhome communities the HOA handles
          the building outside and the owner handles the inside, but the rules
          differ, so ask. Take photos before anything is moved, and tell the HOA
          in writing if the water could reach a neighbor. Then call us for the
          drying. Ask your insurer too, since an HOA policy and your own policy
          cover different things.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          Water is coming in under the door of my ground-floor condo during a
          hard rain. What do I do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Stay away from wet outlets, and do not wade through the water. It is
          outside water and can be dirty. Take photos of the water line on the
          walls and the floor. Tell your property manager. Then call. Outside
          water is often covered differently from a pipe leak, so ask your
          insurer what your policy says.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          A pipe burst in my apartment building and the whole building lost water.
          What do I do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Ask the property manager how long it will last, and whether a
          restoration company has been called for the building. Take photos of
          any wet floor or ceiling in your own unit, even if it looks small. Do
          not run the taps or flush if the building is told to stay off the
          water. If your unit has standing water or a sagging ceiling, call us.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My neighbor's leak is coming through my ceiling and they are not home.
          What now?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Take photos, and write down the time you first saw it. Call your
          property manager or HOA and ask them to shut off the water to the unit
          above. Stay out from under a sagging ceiling. If the water keeps
          coming, call. Do not try to enter the other unit yourself.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="duluth" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/suwanee">Suwanee</a> ·
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/lilburn">Lilburn</a> ·
      <a href="/buford">Buford</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
